  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 204 PM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nThe long term forecast sees little in the way of change with this \nupdate. The beginning of the week through Wednesday still looks to \nbe warm as the upper level jet remains to the north of the area and \nour current air mass warms a bit thanks to copious sunshine. High \ntemperatures Tuesday and Wednesday will be 5 to 10 degrees F above \naverage, which corresponds to temperatures in the 70s for most (a \nfew 60s in far north Georgia, especially at elevation).\n\nUncertainty remains going into Thursday regarding our precipitation \nchances. The ensemble trend for this forecast period was to pull the \nmain surface pressure falls further to the north across the average. \nIntensification of the jet streak within the broad trough \nforecast to dig into the eastern CONUS doesn't happen in the mean \nuntil after the axis pushes in overhead. K-mean clustering \nalongside some analysis of individual members shows short waves \nprogressing through the western side of the trough in a more \nunbiased manner, then phasing with the larger trough going into \nlate Thursday night and Friday morning. The overall net effect is \nto push any PoPs mostly across northern Georgia on Thursday \nafternoon and evening that would be associated with the push of a \nfront into the area. Given the complex nature of the upper level \nflow details, I'd expect PoPs to continue to adjust a bit run to \nrun over the next few days.\n\nCold front pushes through the area into Friday. Forecast highs on \nFriday are 5-10 degrees below average now, as it does look like the \nfront will progress through most all of north and central Georgia by \nthe afternoon. That brings us on of the cooler days of the year so \nfar, only getting into the 50s and low 60s (and some high 40s across \nfar north Georgia). A hard freeze is likely coming for portions of \nnorth Georgia Friday night into Saturday morning, and again on \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. Lows in north Georgia should be \nin the upper 20s, with low 30s into the metro and Athens and mid 30s \ninto central Georgia.\n\nOne additional thing that may need to start being mentioned - the \npotential for some mountain precip early Friday morning in NE \nGeorgia. Current forecast temps aren't quite cold enough for any \nsnow mention - still a bit early in the season. But worth keeping an \neye on in the highest elevations, as a small swing in the forecast \ncould bring some early season chances in. Would put the overall \nprobability very low at this time (under 10%).",
